=== B. The Cocktail Effect and Limits of Classical Single-Compound Toxicology
<b.-the-cocktail-effect-and-limits-of-classical-single-compound-toxicology>
Regulatory bodies (EFSA, FDA) evaluate toxicity molecule by molecule, determining an Acceptable Daily Intake (ADI) for each isolated additive. However, modern consumers ingest a daily blend of 15 to 30 distinct additives in ultra-processed diets. This creates an unstudied "cocktail effect": synergistic interactions where the simultaneous presence of emulsifiers (E466, polysorbates) permeabilizes the intestinal epithelial barrier, allowing increased systemic absorption of heavy metals, preservatives (nitrites), and pesticide residues. The home cook avoids this hazard entirely by building meals around unformulated, raw whole ingredients.

